# Target C-II — dominant retained O--O pair weight stays linear through `t<=1.865`

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` quantitative extension
**Scope:** full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with the corrected `g=0.0076` matched atomic floor
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited shared-retention/complement-incidence inequalities; retained dominant pair-weight interpretation
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_dominant_double_outer_trains_g0076_t1865.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The dominant retained-mass bootstrap used for the old `t<=1.860` O--O theorem is
symbolic in the endpoint `T`.  Its monotonicity proof requires only

\[
U\ge C_{76}>2\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\delta_{max}<1,
\]

not the old numerical value `T=1.860`.  Re-running the same outward-rational bootstrap
at

\[
\boxed{T=1.865}
\]

still leaves a positive retained O--O pair margin.

For every sufficiently large full-core Type-II survivor with

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le1.865,
\]

the retained dominant O--O pair weight satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{P_{OO}^{dom}\over D}>0.0027-o(1).
}
\tag{E65.1}
\]

Since the dominant exact orbit has at most three projective phase classes, one phase
class carries

\[
\boxed{
P_i>0.0009-o(1).
}
\tag{E65.2}
\]

Retained pair weight per train is at most one, so that phase contains more than
`0.0009D-o(D)` actual O--O complete trains.

This extends the existence of a linear dominant O--O family beyond the current
low-energy dual-path endpoint `1.8623`.  It does not itself claim terminal localization
or a dual path.

---

## 1. Parent bootstrap at a symbolic endpoint

Use the parent variables

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2,
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\tag{E65.3}
\]

and

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{E65.4}
\]

The corrected matched atomic theorem gives

\[
U\ge C_{76}-o(1).
\tag{E65.5}
\]

Define

\[
m_2(U)={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2},
\qquad
r(U)=U-m_2(U),
\tag{E65.6}
\]

and

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\tag{E65.7}
\]

The audited retained-mass stack gives

\[
z+p\ge
\pi-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+m_2(U)-o(1),
\tag{E65.8}
\]

\[
p\le
{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1),
\tag{E65.9}
\]

and

\[
\delta\ge F(z)-o(1).
\tag{E65.10}
\]

Fix `T=1.865` and put

\[
U_{max}={9T\over2}-2\pi,
\qquad
\delta_{max}={U_{max}-C_{76}\over2}.
\tag{E65.11}
\]

The exact verifier proves

\[
\boxed{\delta_{max}<0.099<1.}
\tag{E65.12}
\]

It also checks the radical domain

\[
8\Delta_2-U^2>0
\]

at every bootstrap evaluation.

The parent monotonicity remains valid on this interval.  For

\[
U\ge C_{76}>2\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\]

\[
m_2'(U)<0,
\qquad
r'(U)>0.
\tag{E65.13}
\]

Writing

\[
S=3\pi-5t+2\delta+m_2(U),
\]

gives

\[
S_t<0,
\qquad
S_\delta>0,
\qquad
S_\kappa>0.
\tag{E65.14}
\]

These are exactly the sign inputs used in the original `t<=1.860` theorem.

---

## 2. Exact rounded bootstrap

If `z>=z_j-o(1)`, put

\[
d_j:=F(z_j),
\qquad
U_j:=U_{max}-2d_j.
\]

The parent argument gives the same lower map

\[
\boxed{
z\ge S_j-P_j-o(1),}
\tag{E65.15}
\]

where

\[
S_j=
\pi-U_j-{T\over2}+m_2(U_j),
\tag{E65.16}
\]

and

\[
P_j=
{\delta_{max}-d_j+r(U_j)^2
\over2(1-\delta_{max})}.
\tag{E65.17}
\]

The exact certificate uses Machin bounds for `pi`, integer-square-root enclosures, and
alternating-series enclosures for `sin`.  It proves

```text
0       -> z > 1.9111
1.9111  -> z > 2.0144
2.0144  -> z > 2.0311
2.0311  -> z > 2.0339
2.0339  -> z > 2.0344
2.0344  -> z > 2.0345
```

with respective exact margins larger than

```text
8.79e-5,
5.46e-5,
6.29e-5,
1.04e-4,
8.51e-5,
7.09e-5.
```

Hence

\[
\boxed{z>2.0345-o(1).}
\tag{E65.18}
\]

The same exact certificate gives

\[
F(2.0345)>0.03876343775,
\tag{E65.19}
\]

and therefore, from (E65.3),

\[
\boxed{U<2.031787818+o(1).}
\tag{E65.20}
\]

---

## 3. Convert retained occurrence mass to retained O--O pair weight

Freeze shrinking windows around the dominant exact three-phase orbit.  For every
complete train `tau`, let `a_tau,b_tau in[0,1]` be its two retained terminal-occurrence
weights and put

\[
w_\tau:=\min(a_\tau,b_\tau).
\]

Finitely,

\[
a_\tau+b_\tau\le1+w_\tau.
\]

Summing over dominant-window trains gives the established pair-weight inequality

\[
\boxed{z\le M_{dom}+P_{OO}^{dom}+o(1),}
\tag{E65.21}
\]

where `M_dom` is normalized dominant complete-train mass and

\[
P_{OO}^{dom}
={1\over D}\sum_{\tau\,dom}w_\tau.
\]

The exposed-hull train-mass chain gives

\[
M_{dom}\le U+o(1).
\tag{E65.22}
\]

Therefore

\[
P_{OO}^{dom}
\ge z-U-o(1).
\]

Using (E65.18)--(E65.20),

\[
{P_{OO}^{dom}\over D}
>2.0345-2.031787818-o(1)
>0.0027-o(1),
\]

proving (E65.1).

The dominant orbit has at most three projective phase classes, so one phase receives at
least one third of this pair weight.  Thus

\[
P_i>0.0009-o(1),
\]

which proves (E65.2).

---

## 4. Scope and endpoint sharpness of this mechanism

The extension uses no low-energy Markov truncation.  It is therefore independent of
the current `1.8623` low-energy path endpoint.

A numerical fixed-point exploration of the same rounded bootstrap, used only for route
selection, puts the zero of `z-U` near

\[
1.865196.
\]

Thus `T=1.865` is close to the endpoint of the present retained-pair mechanism.  The
exact theorem stops at the safely rounded value `1.865`; no claim is made at
`1.8651` or at the exploratory root.

What is proved:

```text
t <= 1.865
-> dominant retained O-O pair weight >0.0027D-o(D)
-> one shrinking dominant phase carries >0.0009D-o(D) retained pair weight
-> that phase contains >0.0009D-o(D) actual O-O trains.
```

What is not proved here:

- low terminal energy for those trains;
- fixed positive terminal-arc separation;
- a complement-dual path;
- an exclusion for `t>1.865`; or
- C-II closure.

The phase-zero marker route is designed precisely to turn retained pair weight into a
path without a low-energy threshold, but that is a separate theorem and should be
audited separately at the new endpoint.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only retained-pair extension through `t<=1.865`. C-II and
Level I remain open.
